  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 248 AM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nAs Saturday's system departs, mid-level ridging will build in ahead \nof an approaching shortwave. This will leave us on the eastern edge \nof high pressure over the Northern Plains, sandwiched between cool, \ndry air to our north and warm, moist air to our south. This setup, \ncombined with zonal to southwesterly flow aloft, will bring us daily \nchances for showers and storms through much of the coming week. \n\nOf particular note in this active period is the aforementioned \nshortwave. Latest guidance has this wave trending slower, resulting \nin the initial warm front not reaching the area until Monday \nafternoon. This has lead to a decreasing trend in high temperatures \nfor Monday, though highs are still expected to be above-normal due \nto the strong warm advection regime. This will also establish \ninstability across the warm sector supportive of showers and \nthunderstorms. Guidance also suggests that vertical shear will be \nnear 40-50 kts and thus allow for organized convection. This is most \nlikely with forcing along a descending cold front, though \nuncertainty with timing makes it unclear whether this threat would \nbe in the daytime or overnight hours.\n\nAs this frontal boundary passes through the area, it is expected to \nstall across the region. While there is some uncertainty in where \nexactly this boundary will set up, current guidance suggests it will \nbe along our south. This then provides the focus for rainfall over \nthe next several days, especially as southwesterly flow provides a \ncontinuous stream of moisture. Additional waves towards the latter \nhalf of the period will help push this boundary out of the region, \nthough chances for rain will remain.",
